      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1498050108023-c5249f4df085?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Website quotes range from ₱15,000 to ₱500,000+ for seemingly similar projects. Here's what actually drives the price — and how to get the right site at the right budget.",
      content: `
        <p>One of the most common questions we get from small business owners is some version of: "I got three quotes — one for ₱20,000, one for ₱90,000, and one for ₱250,000. They're all for a website. What's going on?" The answer reveals something important about how web projects are scoped — and how to make a smart decision for your business.</p>
        <h2>Why quotes vary so dramatically</h2>
        <p>A website is not a commodity. Two quotes labeled "5-page business website" can represent completely different amounts of work, quality, and long-term value. The ₱20,000 quote is almost certainly a template drop-in with your logo and copy pasted in — functional, but generic. The ₱250,000 quote likely includes strategy, custom design, performance engineering, SEO foundations, and ongoing support. Neither is wrong. They serve different businesses at different stages.</p>
        <p>Price is driven by four factors: <strong>strategy</strong> (how much thinking goes into the structure and goals), <strong>design</strong> (custom vs. template), <strong>development</strong> (off-the-shelf CMS vs. custom build), and <strong>scope</strong> (how many pages, features, and integrations). Understand these and any quote becomes legible.</p>
        <h2>What you get at each price tier</h2>
        <p><strong>Under ₱30,000</strong> — Template-based builds, typically on WordPress or Wix. Minimal customisation. Fast to deliver. Suitable for very early-stage businesses that need a basic online presence and are not yet relying on the website to drive meaningful revenue.</p>
        <p><strong>₱50,000–₱150,000</strong> — Semi-custom design on a solid CMS platform. More strategic input on structure and content. Good performance and SEO foundations. The right range for most established small businesses that want a site they can be proud of and that will actually convert visitors.</p>
        <p><strong>₱200,000+</strong> — Fully custom design and development, deep brand integration, complex features (booking systems, e-commerce, member areas), and typically an ongoing support agreement. Appropriate for businesses where the website is a primary sales channel or customer service touchpoint.</p>
        <h2>Questions to ask any agency before you sign</h2>
        <p>Who does the actual work — your team or subcontractors? What does the handover look like — will you own and be able to edit your site? What's included after launch? How is performance measured? A transparent agency answers all of these without hesitation. One that deflects or overcomplicates the answers is a risk.</p>
        <h2>The real cost is not the build — it's the lost revenue</h2>
        <p>A ₱20,000 website that converts at 0.5% and a ₱120,000 website that converts at 2.5% are not close decisions for a business with real traffic and real revenue potential. The cheapest site is rarely the most economical one. Budget for what your business actually needs to grow — not just for what gets you online.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'rebranding-when-and-how',
      title: "When to Rebrand (And When You Really Shouldn't)",
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '25 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434626881859-194d67b2b86f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Rebranding is expensive, disruptive, and often unnecessary. Here's how to honestly assess whether your brand needs a refresh — or whether the real problem is something else entirely.",
      content: `
        <p>Every few months a business owner comes to us and says, "We need a rebrand." Sometimes they're right. More often, what they actually need is better marketing execution, a clearer value proposition, or a new website — not a new logo. Rebranding for the wrong reasons wastes money and resets brand equity you've spent years building.</p>
        <h2>Legitimate reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>Your business has fundamentally changed.</strong> You've pivoted to a different market, expanded your service offering significantly, or merged with another company. The brand no longer describes what you actually do or who you serve. This is the clearest signal that a rebrand is warranted.</p>
        <p><strong>Your audience has changed.</strong> You started targeting small local clients and now you're pitching enterprise contracts. Your brand aesthetic and voice need to match where you're going, not where you've been.</p>
        <p><strong>Your brand is genuinely creating friction.</strong> Prospects regularly misunderstand your positioning. Your name is being confused with a competitor. You're in a new market where your brand carries no meaning. These are real, measurable problems that a rebrand can solve.</p>
        <h2>Bad reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>You're bored with it.</strong> You've looked at your logo 10,000 times and you're tired of it. Your customers haven't. Brand fatigue is an internal problem — don't solve it at the expense of the recognition you've built externally.</p>
        <p><strong>Sales are down.</strong> A rebrand is not a sales strategy. If revenue is declining, the causes are almost always in your offer, pricing, sales process, or marketing channels — not your logo. Fix the real problem first.</p>
        <p><strong>A competitor rebranded.</strong> Their reasons and yours are different. Don't let a competitor's decision push you into an expensive exercise that doesn't serve your business.</p>
        <h2>The middle path: a brand refresh</h2>
        <p>Most businesses don't need a full rebrand — they need a refresh. Updating the typography, tightening the colour palette, modernising the logo mark without abandoning it, and clarifying the brand voice. This preserves the equity you have while bringing the brand up to date. It's faster, cheaper, and usually more strategically sound than starting from zero.</p>
        <h2>Before you decide, test the hypothesis</h2>
        <p>Ask ten customers — not colleagues, not family — what words come to mind when they think of your brand. Ask what they think you do. Ask if they'd recommend you. Their answers will tell you whether a brand problem actually exists. If it doesn't, spend your rebrand budget on marketing instead.</p>
