      title: "WordPress vs Custom Build: Which Is Right for Your Business?",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '10 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '6 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "It's not a technical decision — it's a business one. Here's the honest breakdown of when WordPress wins and when a custom build is worth the investment.",
      content: `
        <p>We build on both WordPress and custom stacks — Astro, Next.js, and fully bespoke solutions — and we have no agenda either way. What we do have is a clear view of which approach serves which kind of business. Here's the honest breakdown.</p>
        <h2>The case for WordPress</h2>
        <p>WordPress powers around 43% of all websites for a reason: it's mature, well-supported, and editable by anyone. For businesses that need to update their own content regularly — adding blog posts, changing service descriptions, updating team pages — WordPress's admin interface is genuinely easy to use without technical knowledge.</p>
        <p>The plugin ecosystem is massive. Need a booking system, a payment gateway, a contact form with conditional logic, or a membership area? There's a plugin for it, and it typically costs a fraction of what a custom-built equivalent would. For small businesses with standard requirements, WordPress is almost always the faster, more affordable path.</p>
        <h2>Where WordPress falls short</h2>
        <p>Performance is WordPress's biggest liability. A poorly optimised WordPress site — and most are, straight out of the box — scores poorly on Core Web Vitals, loads slowly on mobile, and requires ongoing maintenance to stay secure. Plugins conflict. Updates break things. Hosting matters enormously and most budget hosts are inadequate for anything beyond a basic brochure site.</p>
        <p>WordPress also creates dependency on its own ecosystem. When you need something it doesn't do well — complex data relationships, real-time features, highly customised user experiences — you're fighting the platform rather than building with it.</p>
        <h2>The case for a custom build</h2>
        <p>A custom build starts from nothing and builds exactly what you need — no more. This means cleaner code, better performance, no unnecessary plugin weight, and complete design freedom. For businesses where the website is a core product (SaaS platforms, marketplaces, booking systems with complex logic), custom is not a luxury — it's a requirement.</p>
        <p>Static site generators like Astro produce near-perfect performance scores by default. For marketing sites and content-heavy platforms where speed directly impacts conversions and SEO, this matters enormously. A custom Astro build consistently outperforms a WordPress site on every Core Web Vitals metric.</p>
        <h2>The honest recommendation</h2>
        <p>For most small business brochure sites, service businesses, and content blogs: WordPress, built well, with quality hosting. For e-commerce beyond basic WooCommerce: evaluate purpose-built platforms like Shopify first. For marketing sites where performance and SEO are priorities, or for any business where the website IS the product: custom build. When in doubt, ask what you'll need to change in 12 months. The answer usually makes the decision obvious.</p>
      `,
