      content: `
        <p>Every week we speak with business owners who have been burned by a previous web project: a freelancer who disappeared halfway, an agency that held the domain hostage, a site that looked fine on a laptop but broke on every phone. Choosing the right web design partner in the Philippines is less about finding the cheapest quote and more about avoiding the expensive mistakes. Here is how to evaluate any agency, including us.</p>
        <h2>Look past the portfolio screenshots</h2>
        <p>A portfolio shows what an agency can make look good in a screenshot. It does not show whether those sites load quickly, rank on Google or bring in customers. Ask for <strong>live links</strong>, then open them on your phone using mobile data. Are they fast? Do the forms work? Is the copy clear? Then ask one or two of those clients directly how the project went. A confident agency will happily connect you.</p>
        <h2>Ask who will actually do the work</h2>
        <p>Some agencies sell the project with senior people, then hand it to a junior or an outsourced team you never meet. That is not automatically bad, but you deserve to know. Ask who your day-to-day contact will be, who designs, who develops, and whether any part is subcontracted. <strong>Clarity here predicts how smoothly the project will run.</strong></p>
        <h2>Make sure you will own everything</h2>
        <p>This is the single most important question, and the one most often skipped. Your domain should be registered in your name, on an account you control. You should have admin access to your hosting and your website. Design files, logos and content should belong to you once paid for. If an agency insists on owning the domain "for convenience", walk away. Untangling that later can take months.</p>
        <h2>Compare quotes on scope, not price</h2>
        <p>Quotes for a "business website" can range from ₱15,000 to well over ₱300,000, and both extremes can be honest. The difference is scope. Ask every agency to itemise: number of pages, custom design or template, copywriting included or not, SEO setup, mobile testing, revisions, training, and post-launch support. Lay the quotes side by side and the price gaps usually make sense.</p>
        <p>Watch for red flags: no written contract, 100% payment upfront, vague timelines like "around one to two months", or no mention of what happens after launch.</p>
        <h2>Check how they handle timelines and communication</h2>
        <p>Ask for a project timeline with specific milestones. Ask how often you will get updates and on which channel. Ask what they need from you and by when, because most delayed projects are delayed waiting for client content. A good agency is upfront about that and builds a process around it.</p>
        <h2>Ask what happens after launch</h2>
        <p>A website is not a one-time purchase. Plugins need updates, security needs monitoring, and your content will change. Find out whether the agency offers maintenance, what it costs, and how quickly they respond if the site goes down. An agency that only talks about the build and never about the next twelve months is telling you something.</p>
        <h2>A simple scoring checklist</h2>
        <p>Before deciding, score each agency out of five on: quality of live work, transparency on who does the work, ownership terms, clarity of scope, timeline commitment, and post-launch support. The highest score, not the lowest price, is usually the right choice.</p>
        <p>We built our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> around exactly these principles: fixed scope, fixed price, a clear seven-day timeline, and you own everything from day one. Compare it against any other quote you have.</p>
