    {
      slug: 'website-maintenance-guide',
      title: "Website Maintenance for Small Businesses: What It Costs and Why Skipping It Is Expensive",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '13 Aug 2026',
      readTime: '3 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Outdated plugins, expired SSL and broken forms quietly cost small businesses leads every month. Here's what website maintenance involves and what it should cost.",
      content: `
        <p>Most small business websites are launched with enthusiasm and then left alone. For a while, nothing seems wrong. Then a contact form silently stops sending emails, a plugin update breaks the layout, or the site gets flagged as unsafe because the SSL certificate expired. By the time someone notices, weeks of leads are gone. Maintenance is not glamorous, but it is the cheapest insurance your website will ever have.</p>
        <h2>What website maintenance actually includes</h2>
        <p>A proper maintenance routine covers five areas. <strong>Software updates</strong>: the CMS core, themes and plugins. <strong>Security</strong>: monitoring for malware, blocking brute-force login attempts and keeping SSL valid. <strong>Backups</strong>: automated, stored off-site and tested so they can actually be restored. <strong>Performance</strong>: checking load speed and fixing regressions. <strong>Functional checks</strong>: confirming forms, checkout, booking tools and links still work.</p>
        <h2>Why WordPress sites need it most</h2>
        <p>WordPress powers a huge share of the web, which makes it the most targeted platform for automated attacks. The majority of compromised WordPress sites are not hacked through WordPress itself but through outdated plugins. A site with fifteen plugins, none updated in a year, is an open door. Monthly updates, tested on a staging copy first, close most of those doors.</p>
        <p>Static and modern framework sites, like the ones we build with Astro, have a much smaller attack surface because there is no database or admin panel exposed to the public. They still need dependency updates and monitoring, but the risk and the effort are lower.</p>
        <h2>The hidden costs of skipping maintenance</h2>
        <p><strong>Lost leads</strong> are the biggest cost and the hardest to see. A broken form does not announce itself; enquiries simply stop. <strong>Cleanup after a hack</strong> typically costs far more than a year of maintenance, and if Google flags your site as dangerous, recovering your rankings can take weeks. <strong>Emergency rebuilds</strong> happen when a site has been neglected so long that updating it safely is no longer possible.</p>
        <h2>What maintenance should cost</h2>
        <p>In the Philippine market, basic maintenance plans for a small business website generally range from around ₱2,500 to ₱8,000 per month, depending on the platform, the number of plugins, and how many content updates are included. International agencies typically charge more. Be wary of plans far below this range: they often mean automated updates with no testing, which can break your site just as easily as neglect.</p>
        <h2>A monthly checklist you can follow yourself</h2>
        <p>If you manage your own site, block out one hour a month. Take a full backup. Update plugins and themes one at a time, checking the site after each. Submit every form on the site and confirm the email arrives. Test the site on your phone. Check Google Search Console for errors. Confirm your SSL certificate and domain are set to auto-renew. Write down anything unusual.</p>
        <h2>When to hand it over</h2>
        <p>If your website generates real revenue, if you have more than a handful of plugins, or if you simply never get around to that monthly hour, it is time to delegate. The goal is a site that quietly works every day while you run the business.</p>
        <p>Every site launched through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> comes with a clean, lightweight build that needs far less upkeep, and optional maintenance plans so you never have to think about it.</p>
      `,
