    {
      slug: 'homepage-copywriting-that-sells',
      title: "Homepage Copywriting: How to Write Website Copy That Turns Visitors Into Customers",
      category: 'Strategy',
      date: '30 Jul 2026',
      readTime: '3 min read',
      author: 'Reancirl Balaba',
      authorRole: 'Co-Founder',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/117749302_4540601115965165_6039017566881807039_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434626881859-194d67b2b86f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most homepages talk about the business instead of the customer. Here's a simple, proven structure for homepage copy that makes visitors understand, trust and act.",
      content: `
        <p>You have a few seconds to convince a visitor they are in the right place. Most small business homepages waste them with headlines like "Welcome to Our Website" or "Excellence in Every Detail". These say nothing about what the business does or why anyone should care. Good homepage copy is not clever. It is clear, specific and built around the customer's problem.</p>
        <h2>The headline: say what you do and who it is for</h2>
        <p>Your headline has one job: tell visitors exactly what you offer, in words they would use themselves. A useful test is whether a stranger could repeat back what you do after reading it once. "Aircon cleaning and repair for homes and offices in Iligan City" beats "Your Comfort, Our Commitment" every time. Underneath, a short subheadline can add the key benefit or differentiator: same-day service, fixed pricing, ten-year warranty.</p>
        <h2>Lead with the customer's problem</h2>
        <p>People do not buy services; they buy solutions to problems. Early on the page, name the problem your customer has in their own language. "Tired of contractors who never show up on time?" or "Spending hours every week answering the same customer questions?" When visitors see their own frustration described accurately, they keep reading because they feel understood.</p>
        <h2>Write benefits, then back them with features</h2>
        <p>A feature is what something is. A benefit is what it does for the customer. "Cloud-based booking system" is a feature. "Customers book appointments at 11pm without calling you" is a benefit. Lead with the benefit, then mention the feature that makes it possible. <strong>Every sentence on your homepage should answer the visitor's silent question: what is in it for me?</strong></p>
        <h2>Prove it with specifics</h2>
        <p>Claims are cheap; evidence persuades. Replace vague superlatives with concrete proof. Instead of "trusted by many clients", write "trusted by 140+ local businesses since 2018". Add two or three short testimonials with real names and, ideally, photos. Show logos of clients or certifications. Mention numbers wherever they are honest and impressive: years in business, projects completed, average response time.</p>
        <h2>Make the next step obvious</h2>
        <p>Every homepage needs one primary call to action, repeated at natural points down the page. Make it specific and low-pressure: "Get a free quote in 24 hours" works better than "Submit". Avoid offering five different actions of equal weight; choice paralysis is real. If you have a secondary action, like browsing your work, style it less prominently.</p>
        <h2>Cut ruthlessly</h2>
        <p>Once you have a draft, remove every sentence that does not help a visitor understand, trust or act. Kill the corporate filler: "solutions", "synergy", "world-class", "one-stop shop". Use short paragraphs and plain words. Read it aloud. If you would not say it to a customer face to face, rewrite it.</p>
        <h2>A simple homepage structure</h2>
        <p>Headline and subheadline. Primary call to action. The problem. Your solution and its key benefits. Proof: testimonials, numbers, logos. How it works in three steps. A final call to action. This structure is not fancy, but it works across almost every industry.</p>
        <p>Copy and design work best when built together. Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> includes conversion-focused homepage structure and copy guidance, so your new site sells from the first visit.</p>
