    {
      slug: 'mobile-first-web-design',
      title: "Mobile-First Web Design: Why Your Website Must Be Built for Phones First",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '18 Jun 2026',
      readTime: '3 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497091071254-cc9b2ba7c48a?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most of your customers will see your website on a phone. Here's what mobile-first design really means, why Google cares, and how to get it right for your business.",
      content: `
        <p>For most small businesses, the majority of website visitors now arrive on a phone, and in the Philippines that share is especially high. Yet many websites are still designed on a large desktop monitor and then squeezed down to fit a phone as an afterthought. The result is cramped layouts, tiny buttons and slow pages. Mobile-first design flips the process, and it is no longer optional.</p>
        <h2>What mobile-first actually means</h2>
        <p>Responsive design means a site adapts to different screen sizes. Mobile-first goes further: you <strong>design and build for the smallest screen first</strong>, deciding what truly matters, then progressively enhance the layout for tablets and desktops. Starting small forces clarity. When you only have a narrow column to work with, you have to decide what your visitor needs most and cut everything else.</p>
        <h2>Google ranks the mobile version of your site</h2>
        <p>Google uses mobile-first indexing, which means it primarily looks at the mobile version of your website when deciding how to rank it. If content, headings or structured data are missing on mobile, or the mobile experience is poor, your rankings suffer on every device, including desktop. A beautiful desktop site with a broken mobile version is, from Google's perspective, a broken site.</p>
        <h2>Design for thumbs, not cursors</h2>
        <p>On a phone, people navigate with their thumb, often one-handed. That changes everything. Buttons should be large enough to tap comfortably, generally at least 44 pixels tall, with space between them. Place your primary call to action where thumbs naturally reach. Make phone numbers tappable to call and addresses tappable to open in maps. Avoid hover effects as the only way to reveal important information, because there is no hover on a touchscreen.</p>
        <h2>Prioritise speed on mobile data</h2>
        <p>Mobile visitors are frequently on slower or patchy connections. Every heavy image, video background and script costs more on mobile than on office Wi-Fi. Mobile-first design means compressing images aggressively, avoiding autoplay videos, loading only what the first screen needs and deferring everything else. A site that loads in two seconds on a phone will outperform a prettier one that takes eight.</p>
        <h2>Keep content readable</h2>
        <p>Use a body text size of at least 16 pixels so visitors never need to zoom. Keep paragraphs short. Use clear headings so people can scan. Ensure strong contrast between text and background, especially for people reading outdoors in bright sunlight. Simplify navigation into a clear menu with only the most important pages.</p>
        <h2>Forms and checkout need extra care</h2>
        <p>Typing on a phone is tedious. Ask only for information you truly need. Use the correct input types so phones show a number pad for phone numbers and an email keyboard for email addresses. Enable autofill. For many local businesses, offering a Messenger or click-to-call option alongside the form captures leads who would otherwise give up.</p>
        <h2>How to test your own site</h2>
        <p>Do not rely on shrinking your desktop browser window. Test on real phones, both a recent model and an older budget Android, using mobile data rather than Wi-Fi. Try to complete your most important task, such as booking, buying or enquiring, using only your thumb. Every point of friction you feel, your customers feel too.</p>
        <p>Every website built through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> is designed mobile-first from the very first sketch, tested on real devices, and optimised to load fast on mobile data.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'small-business-website-checklist',
      title: "Small Business Website Checklist: 15 Must-Haves Before You Launch",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '06 Jun 2026',
      readTime: '3 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1702360174953-b9d72d5ddba2?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Launching a new business website? Use this 15-point checklist covering pages, SEO, speed, trust and tracking to make sure nothing important is missed.",
      content: `
        <p>A new website launch is exciting, and that excitement is exactly when important details get missed. We have seen sites go live with contact forms sending to an old email address, no analytics installed and pages still blocked from Google. Use this 15-point checklist before launch, whether you built the site yourself or hired an agency.</p>
        <h2>Essential pages</h2>
        <p><strong>1. A clear homepage</strong> that states what you do, who you serve and what visitors should do next, all visible without scrolling. <strong>2. Individual service pages</strong> for each main offering, rather than one long page listing everything. <strong>3. An about page</strong> with real photos of you and your team; people buy from people. <strong>4. A contact page</strong> with a form, phone number, email, address, embedded map and business hours. <strong>5. Privacy policy and terms</strong>, required if you collect any personal data through forms, and expected by customers and advertising platforms.</p>
        <h2>Trust and conversion</h2>
        <p><strong>6. Social proof</strong> such as testimonials, reviews, client logos or case studies, placed near your calls to action. <strong>7. A clear call to action on every page</strong>, so no page is a dead end. <strong>8. Working forms</strong>: submit every form yourself and confirm the email arrives in the right inbox, not in spam. <strong>9. A professional email address</strong> on your own domain, which looks far more credible than a free webmail address.</p>
        <h2>SEO foundations</h2>
        <p><strong>10. Unique title tags and meta descriptions</strong> on every page, written to include the page's main keyword. <strong>11. Search Console and a sitemap</strong>: verify your site in Google Search Console, submit your XML sitemap, and double-check that no page is accidentally set to "noindex", a common leftover from development. <strong>12. Correct heading structure</strong>, with one H1 per page and logical H2s beneath it, plus descriptive alt text on images.</p>
        <h2>Speed, mobile and security</h2>
        <p><strong>13. Mobile testing on real devices.</strong> Check every page on at least one iPhone and one Android phone. Tap every button, open every menu, and fill in every form. <strong>14. Performance and SSL.</strong> Run PageSpeed Insights and fix major issues, especially oversized images. Confirm your SSL certificate is active so the site loads with HTTPS and no browser warnings, and that the non-www and www versions redirect to a single address.</p>
        <h2>Tracking and measurement</h2>
        <p><strong>15. Analytics and conversion tracking.</strong> Install an analytics tool before launch, not after, so you have data from day one. Set up goals or events for the actions that matter: form submissions, phone clicks, bookings and purchases. Without this, you will never know which pages and channels actually bring in business.</p>
        <h2>After launch: the first 30 days</h2>
        <p>Launch is the beginning, not the end. In the first month, monitor Search Console for crawl errors, watch analytics to see which pages visitors use and where they leave, ask your first customers for feedback, and request Google reviews. Small adjustments in the first few weeks, based on real behaviour, often produce the biggest improvements.</p>
        <h2>Print it, tick it, launch it</h2>
        <p>Go through the list one item at a time and do not launch until every box is ticked. Most items take minutes to check, and each one prevents a problem that could quietly cost you customers for months.</p>
        <p>Prefer to skip the checklist entirely? Every item above is included as standard in our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a>, tested and verified before your site goes live.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'why-brand-identity-matters',
      title: 'Why Brand Identity Is Your Most Important Business Asset',
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '18 Apr 2025',
      readTime: '6 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434626881859-194d67b2b86f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "In a market flooded with options, the brands that win are the ones people remember. Here's why investing in identity is always worth it.",
      content: `
        <p>In a world where every category is crowded, the brand that wins isn't always the best product — it's the one people remember. Brand identity is the system that makes remembering easy.</p>
        <h2>What brand identity actually is</h2>
        <p>Brand identity isn't just a logo. It's the complete visual and verbal language your business uses to communicate with the world — your logo, typography, colour palette, photography style, tone of voice, and the way all of these work together across every touchpoint.</p>
        <p>When done well, brand identity creates immediate recognition, builds trust over time, and makes every marketing dollar work harder. When done poorly — or not at all — you're starting from zero every time someone encounters you.</p>
        <h2>The ROI argument</h2>
        <p>Businesses with strong, consistent brand identities outperform their less-defined competitors on every measurable metric: revenue, customer loyalty, price resilience, and employee retention. The research is unambiguous.</p>
        <p>Lucidpress found that consistent brand presentation increases revenue by 23% on average. McKinsey's Brand Power study shows that strong brands deliver three times the shareholder value of weak ones. These aren't soft, vanity metrics — they're business outcomes.</p>
        <h2>The compounding effect</h2>
        <p>The real power of brand identity is that it compounds. Every piece of content, every ad, every email that carries your brand consistently adds to a mental bank account in your audience's mind. Over months and years, this creates the kind of brand recognition that makes people choose you instinctively — without even comparing options.</p>
        <p>This is why the businesses that invest in proper identity work early almost always outgrow those who patch it together as they go. The foundation determines everything built on top of it.</p>
        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <p>If your brand identity is inconsistent, unclear, or simply doesn't reflect who you are today, the answer isn't to keep patching — it's to rebuild properly. Start with strategy: who are you, who do you serve, what do you believe, and what makes you different? From that foundation, design can speak with clarity and conviction.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'core-web-vitals-seo',
      title: 'Core Web Vitals: What They Are and Why They Destroy Rankings',
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '02 Apr 2025',
      readTime: '8 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
